-- title:
--   A fixed element in one of two swapped ideals pins both
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring and $\gamma : S \simeq_{+*} S$ a ring automorphism of $S$, and let $P_1, P_2$ be ideals of $S$. Assume that $\gamma$ permutes the pair $\{P_1, P_2\}$ in the weak sense that the image ideal $\gamma(P_1)$ (the ideal generated by the image of $P_1$ under the underlying ring homomorphism) equals $P_1$ or equals $P_2$, and likewise $\gamma(P_2)$ equals $P_1$ or equals $P_2$. Assume further that there is an element $a \in S$ fixed by $\gamma$, i.e. $\gamma(a) = a$, which lies in $P_1$ but not in $P_2$. Then both ideals are individually stable: $\gamma(P_1) = P_1$ and $\gamma(P_2) = P_2$. No primality, finiteness or Noetherian hypothesis is imposed on $S$ or on the ideals, and the two alternatives in the hypotheses are not assumed exclusive.
--
--   This is the elementary rigidity statement that a $\gamma$-fixed element distinguishing two ideals forbids the automorphism from interchanging them, so that each ideal is separately stable. It is used in the analysis of a crossing model, where Galois stability of each of the two branches individually is deduced from the existence of elements pinning them.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_map_eq_self_of_apply_eq_of_mem_of_not_mem.lean

